  "published": "2026-11-10T21:01:22.21Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Breaking changes:\n- The packages no longer have a `net8.0` build. An application on .NET 8 or .NET 9 gets a .NET Standard build, without the APIs that only the `net10.0` build has, such as `DateOnly` and `TimeOnly` support. To keep those APIs, move the application to .NET 10.\n- The `netstandard1.x` and `netstandard2.0` builds no longer list `JetBrains.Annotations` as a NuGet dependency. If your code uses its attributes and you got the package through DataStandardizer, add a reference to `JetBrains.Annotations` to your project.\n\nFixed:\n- Debuggers can step into the packages' source through Source Link.\n- `CompareTo` on the ISO 639 code types (`Iso639Part1Language`, `Iso639Part2BLanguage`, `Iso639Part2TLanguage`, `Iso639Part3Language` and `Iso639Part5LanguageFamily`) orders two codes instead of throwing `ArgumentException`, so lists of codes can be sorted.\n- `CompareTo` on an ISO 639 code type returns 1 for `null`, as `IComparable` requires, instead of throwing `ArgumentNullException`.\n- `CompareTo` on a default instance of an ISO 639 code type throws `InvalidOperationException` instead of `NullReferenceException`.\n- On .NET 10, `GetDate`, `GetAge`, `GetAlias`, `GetEnglishName` and `GetFrenchName` on an `Iso15924Script` no longer return a wrong date or throw when the current culture uses a non-Gregorian calendar, such as th-TH or ar-SA.\n\nChanged:\n- The netstandard1.x and netstandard2.0 builds now carry nullable annotations, so projects on C# 8 or later with nullable enabled get null warnings from them.",
